  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 356 PM EDT TUE OCT 6 2026\n\nThe extended period opens with quiet conditions, fair skies, and\nlight winds through the remainder of the work week as surface \nhigh pressure remains near eastern Kentucky through Thursday \nbefore starting to shift towards the Mid-Atlantic and Northeast on\nFriday. This will keep dry weather intact across the region, with\nunseasonably warm afternoon highs reaching the upper 70s to lower\n80s on both Thursday and Friday. Clear skies and light winds will\nfavor ridge-valley temperature splits each night. Temperatures \neach night should drop into the mid-to-upper 40s in the coldest, \nsheltered valleys, while remaining in the lower 50s elsewhere.\n\nA significant pattern shift unfolds over the weekend as an\nanomalous upper-level ridge amplifies across the Eastern US,\nraising 500 mb heights over the Commonwealth toward 584-588 dam\nby Saturday. The vast majority of the JKL CWA will remain warm\nand mostly dry through Saturday afternoon, with highs reaching the\nupper 70s to lower 80s. However, all eyes remain on a developing\ntropical disturbance in the southwestern Gulf (Invest 92L). As\nlow-level flow turns southerly around the departing high, deep\nmoisture associated with Invest 92L will begin creeping\nnorthward. PWATs will climb toward 1.0-1.2 inches near the\nTennessee border by Saturday evening, bringing low-end shower and\nisolated thunderstorm chances into far southern Kentucky into the\nevening and overnight.\n\nAs the system lifts northeast through the Lower Mississippi and\nTennessee Valleys, rain is likely across eastern Kentucky through\nSunday afternoon, accompanied by isolated to scattered thunderstorm\nchances. Rainfall amounts remain highly uncertain, as there is a\ngreat deal of uncertainty in regards to the strength and\npositioning of the system, and how it might interact with an\napproaching surface cold front pushed by northern stream energy\npassing through the Great Lakes. Broad southwesterly flow aloft\nbetween the Atlantic ridge and western troughing will help channel\nthe tropical moisture plume from the Gulf low into the Ohio Valley.\nThis will surge PWAT values across the JKL CWA Sunday morning into\nearly Sunday afternoon. Forecasted PWATs currently fall within the\n90th percentile. However, the latest 12Z GFS guidance has trended\ndownward for rainfall across the area. PWATs do drop off\nnoticeably Monday into Tuesday as the primary moisture plume axis\nshifts east of the area. As a cold front sags slowly into the\nregion, shower chances will linger into early next week, though\ndecreasing moisture efficiency will mitigate the threat for heavy\nrainfall after Sunday. Extensive cloud cover and rain will keep\ndaytime highs in the low-to-mid 70s Sunday and Monday. Height\nrises and fair skies will allow temperatures to warm into the\nupper 70s Tuesday, while overnight lows cool into the\nmid-to-upper 50s throughout the long term period.",
